# 12. End-to-end release scope

A release is complete only when all of the following work together:

1. Application boots to a polished title/start screen.
2. New game and continue game paths work.
3. Player spawns at a valid location with functioning third-person controls.
4. The starting region is fully traversable within its defined boundary.
5. Collision, interaction, and contextual prompts work.
6. Residents execute schedules and local rule-based behaviors.
7. NPC conversations and relationship changes work through authored logic.
8. Day/night and at least the region's selected weather states function.
9. At least one vehicle can be entered, driven, exited, and saved safely.
10. Supported interiors can be entered and exited without losing the player state.
11. Mission/activity objectives, completion, rewards, and journal updates work.
12. Region travel loads and unloads assets safely.
13. Other regions have their defined first-release activities and residents.
14. Save, autosave, resume, export, and import work.
15. Settings and quality tiers work.
16. No API key, cloud model, backend, account, or remote database is required.
17. Automated tests cover simulation rules, mission transitions, save migrations, and core navigation/state behavior.
18. Browser end-to-end tests cover boot, new game, movement, interaction, driving, mission completion, save, reload, and resume.
19. A target-laptop profiling pass records frame time, memory behavior, and transition stability.
20. Asset licenses and attribution are recorded.

# 13. Delivery plan

## Stage 0 — Specification approval and technical feasibility

- Confirm product boundaries and first-release content.
- Inspect the actual development machine and available GPU/browser capability.
- Create a representative asset/performance budget.
- Validate the chosen stack and package versions.
- Produce the implementation plan and acceptance test matrix.

**Exit condition:** approved specification, architecture decisions, and a testable production plan.

## Stage 1 — Engine foundation

- Application boot, input, renderer, camera, scene lifecycle.
- Simulation clock, entity model, event bus, deterministic seed.
- Save schema and basic settings.
- One small graybox test district.

**Exit condition:** stable playable graybox with save/resume and repeatable simulation tests.

## Stage 2 — Solara Bay vertical slice

- Finalized representative art direction.
- Player controller, collisions, interactions.
- A small resident population with schedules, navigation, dialogue, and social state.
- One interior, one vehicle, one activity chain.
- Day/night and a limited weather loop.

**Exit condition:** one polished, complete district proving all major systems together.

## Stage 3 — First-release world content

- Complete Solara Bay.
- Add Vesper City, Verdant Highlands, Aether District, and World Gateway.
- Add region transitions, region-specific activity sets, and interiors.
- Optimize assets and scene streaming.

**Exit condition:** all planned regions are connected and playable.

## Stage 4 — Integration, hardening, and release

- Save migration and recovery.
- Accessibility, input, settings, and UI polish.
- Automated unit, integration, and end-to-end testing.
- Target-laptop profiling and quality tuning.
- License/attribution audit.
- Production build and local release package.
- Optional static hosting only after local release acceptance and explicit approval.

**Exit condition:** all release acceptance criteria pass, with known limitations documented.

# 14. Risks and mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
| --- | --- |
| Photorealistic asset production exceeds time or hardware | Limit district size, use modular kits, optimize hero assets, profile early |
| Scope expands into an unfinishable open world | Freeze first-release region boundaries and content budgets |
| Too many NPCs overload CPU | Tiered simulation, staggered updates, active population limits |
| Vehicle physics becomes a project of its own | Use arcade handling and route-driven traffic first |
| Interiors multiply asset and streaming costs | Only selected interiors are fully explorable |
| Local saves become incompatible | Versioned schema, migrations, export/import validation |
| Third-party asset licenses are unclear | Asset provenance and license gate |
| AI is mistaken for generative AI | Document explicitly: deterministic authored game logic only |
| Browser/GPU differences cause failures | WebGL-compatible baseline, feature detection, adaptive quality |
| A single large codebase becomes tightly coupled | Keep simulation, rendering, gameplay, content, and persistence behind explicit interfaces |

# 15. Explicit exclusions from first release

- Multiplayer, co-op, shared online worlds, and networking.
- Hosted or local LLM dialogue.
- Any API keys, cloud AI endpoints, or required online service.
- Supabase, Firebase, authentication, or a custom backend.
- A seamless planet-sized map or an unlimited number of worlds.
- Full-scale commercial GTA-level content density.
- Weapons, combat, crime, police pursuit, and wanted-level systems unless separately approved.
- Advanced vehicle simulation, vehicle customization, and complex destruction.
- Full interior access to every building.
- Procedural generation of unlimited missions or dialogue.
- VR support.
- Mobile-native packaging; responsive browser controls can be considered after desktop stability.

